        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.343,
            0.457,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "More and more numbers!",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.365,
            0.886,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Recall that the very first numbers we learned about in the study of mathematics were the counting numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, …"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.411,
            0.885,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then we learned that there are even more numbers! For example, there is the number 0 (zero), representing nothing, which comes before 1. The number 0 has a very important history in India and now in the world. For example, around the world we learn to write numbers in the Indian number system using the digits 0 to 9, allowing us to write numbers however large or however small using just these 10 digits."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.548,
            0.884,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We then learned about more numbers that exist between the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, … , such as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{2} , \\quad \\frac{3}{2} , \\quad  and  \\quad \\frac{13}{6}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . These are called fractions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.616,
            0.885,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "But are there still more numbers? Well, 0 is an additional number that we didn’t know about earlier, and it comes before 1 and is less than 1. Are there perhaps more numbers that come before 0 and are less than 0?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.707,
            0.76,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Phrased another way, we have seen the number line:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.738,
            0.812,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.235,
                0.738,
                0.812,
                0.772
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_0_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.778,
            0.885,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, this is actually only a number ‘ray’, in the language we learned earlier in geometry; this ray starts at 0 and goes forever to the right. Do there exist numbers to the left of 0, so that this number ray can be completed to a true number line?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.869,
            0.702,
            0.891
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "That is what we will investigate in this chapter!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 14,
